The purpose of TOGAF is to help enterprises:

translate business strategy into architecture direction;
design current-state and target-state enterprise architecture;
govern change through a repeatable architecture development method;
create transition roadmaps from baseline to target states;
establish architecture principles, standards, and decision rights; and
improve alignment across strategy, delivery, operations, risk, and technology platforms.

TOGAF is especially useful when an organisation needs a disciplined architecture method across business and technology change. Typical situations include:

enterprise transformation and operating model redesign;
modernisation of applications, data platforms, or infrastructure;
digital strategy execution and capability-based planning;
mergers, acquisitions, or organisational restructuring;
cloud migration and platform rationalisation;
development of enterprise standards and architecture governance; and
integration of multiple delivery programmes under one target-state design.

4.3 Architecture Capability and Decision Bodies
A TOGAF-based architecture capability commonly includes:

architecture board or design authority;
chief architect and architecture practice;
domain architects and solution architects;
architecture repository and standards library;
architecture principles and policies;
review, waiver, and compliance processes; and
linkage to portfolio, PMO, risk, security, data, and service management.

5.4 Supporting TOGAF Concepts
TOGAF also includes several important supporting concepts that strengthen the ADM:

Architecture Principles to guide consistent decisions;
Stakeholder Management to align concerns and viewpoints;
Architecture Repository to store standards, models, building blocks, and governance records;
Enterprise Continuum to classify reusable assets from generic to organisation-specific;
Architecture Building Blocks (ABBs) and Solution Building Blocks (SBBs);
Capability-Based Planning to connect transformation to business outcomes;
Gap Analysis between baseline and target states; and
Architecture Compliance Reviews during implementation.

The purpose of PRINCE2 is to help organisations:

justify projects through an ongoing business case;
define roles, authority, and accountability clearly;
control delivery through stages and decision points;
manage scope, risk, quality, issues, and change in an integrated way;
create a repeatable governance model across projects; and
tailor project control to the scale, complexity, risk, and context of the initiative.

PRINCE2 is especially useful when an organisation needs consistent project control and governance across multiple projects or delivery teams. Typical situations include:

formal project delivery in public or regulated environments;
technology and digital transformation projects;
projects requiring strong business sponsorship and stage approval;
multi-vendor or outsourced delivery environments;
organisations seeking a standard method for PMO oversight;
projects where documentation, assurance, and decision traceability matter; and
complex initiatives that need separation between governance and day-to-day management.

4.4 PRINCE2 7 Focus Areas
PRINCE2 7 places additional emphasis on themes that modern projects must handle more explicitly:

people;
digital and data management;
commercial management;
sustainability; and
stronger tailoring to project context.

PeopleCert describes PRINCE2 7 as a major update that introduces stronger focus on people management, digital and data, sustainability, and compatibility with modern delivery environments.

Management of Risk (M_o_R / MoR) is a structured framework for identifying, assessing, responding to, monitoring, and communicating risk across an organisation. Originally developed by the UK Cabinet Office and now stewarded through PeopleCert, it provides a consistent way to manage uncertainty at strategic, portfolio, programme, project, and operational levels.
MoR is not limited to a single project risk register. It is designed to help organisations build a risk-aware culture, define clear governance for risk decisions, and integrate risk management into planning, investment, delivery, operations, and assurance.



The purpose of MoR is to help organisations:

create and protect value by managing uncertainty in a structured way;
improve decision-making through clearer visibility of threats and opportunities;
define consistent risk governance, roles, tolerances, and escalation routes;
embed risk thinking into strategy, change, service delivery, and operations;
strengthen resilience, compliance, and assurance; and
promote a culture where risk is discussed openly and managed proportionately.




MoR is most useful when an organisation:

wants an enterprise-wide risk management approach;
needs a common language for risk across business and technology functions;
runs multiple projects, programmes, and portfolios that require coordinated risk oversight;
faces regulatory, operational, cyber, financial, or transformation risk exposure;
wants to improve governance, escalation, and risk reporting; or
needs stronger linkage between risk appetite, decision-making, and assurance.




MoR is particularly relevant for:

board members and executive committees;
risk committees and enterprise risk functions;
CIOs, CISOs, CFOs, and operations leaders;
portfolio, programme, and project managers;
PMOs, portfolio offices, and governance teams;
audit, compliance, and assurance functions; and
managers responsible for delivery, service continuity, security, or regulatory obligations.




MoR provides a governance model for managing risk consistently across the organisation. It usually works through an integrated set of principles, process steps, documents, roles, and embedded practices.
A practical MoR implementation helps leadership answer key questions such as:

What uncertainty could affect our objectives?
What level of risk are we willing to accept?
Who owns each major risk and response decision?
Are our controls and mitigations effective?
How are risks changing over time across the enterprise?
When should risks be escalated, accepted, transferred, reduced, exploited, or closed?

