faces regulatory, operational, cyber, financial, or transformation risk exposure;
wants to improve governance, escalation, and risk reporting; or
needs stronger linkage between risk appetite, decision-making, and assurance.

MoR is particularly relevant for:

board members and executive committees;
risk committees and enterprise risk functions;
CIOs, CISOs, CFOs, and operations leaders;
portfolio, programme, and project managers;
PMOs, portfolio offices, and governance teams;
audit, compliance, and assurance functions; and
managers responsible for delivery, service continuity, security, or regulatory obligations.

Organisational Perspectives
MoR is frequently applied across four organisational perspectives:

Strategic — risks affecting enterprise direction, policy, reputation, major investment, or long-term objectives;
Portfolio / Programme / Project — risks affecting change investments, transformation, delivery, benefits, and dependencies;
Operational — risks affecting business operations, service continuity, compliance, resilience, and control effectiveness; and
Functional / Specialist — risks managed in specialist domains such as cyber security, finance, legal, procurement, data, or health and safety.

Typical Governance Roles
Common roles in a MoR environment include:

Board / Executive Committee — sets overall risk appetite and oversight expectations;
Risk Committee — reviews major exposures, themes, trends, and control effectiveness;
Chief Risk Officer / Risk Lead — defines policy, methods, and enterprise-level coordination;
Risk Owners — are accountable for specific risks and response decisions;
Risk Action Owners — are responsible for implementing agreed responses or controls;
Portfolio / Programme / Project Managers — manage delivery-related risks and escalations;
PMO / Portfolio Office / Governance Office — supports reporting, standards, data quality, and review cycles; and
Audit / Compliance / Assurance Functions — provide challenge, review, and confidence assessment.

Decision Forums and Control Points
Typical MoR governance involves recurring forums and control points such as:

board and risk committee reviews;
portfolio and investment reviews;
programme and project board meetings;
operational risk and resilience reviews;
control self-assessment and compliance reviews;
incident and issue review meetings; and
audit and assurance checkpoints.

Typical MoR Artefact Families
A mature MoR implementation normally produces artefacts across several categories:

policy and governance documents;
risk identification and assessment artefacts;
response and control artefacts;
reporting and escalation artefacts; and
assurance and improvement artefacts.

Typical examples include:

risk management policy;
risk management process guide;
risk appetite statement;
risk tolerance thresholds;
enterprise risk taxonomy;
strategic risk register;
portfolio, programme, project, or operational risk registers;
risk assessment matrix;
control library or control catalogue;
key risk indicator dashboard;
escalation log;
issue and incident linkage records;
assurance review pack; and
lessons learned and improvement log.

Critical Success Factors
Successful MoR adoption usually depends on:

visible executive sponsorship;
simple and proportionate methods;
alignment with strategy, finance, audit, and assurance;
strong quality of risk data and reporting;
active ownership rather than passive register maintenance; and
consistent follow-through on risk responses and escalations.

The purpose of ITIL 4 is to help organisations:

align services with business outcomes and customer value;
establish a common operating model for design, transition, support, and improvement;
improve reliability, responsiveness, and service experience;
create consistent governance and accountability across service teams;
support digital transformation through adaptable practices rather than rigid process bureaucracy; and
embed continual improvement into daily operations and strategic change.

ITIL 4 is especially useful when an organisation needs a coherent service management model across operations, support, delivery, and supplier ecosystems. Common situations include:

establishing or improving IT service management;
modernising service desk, incident, problem, and change practices;
introducing service ownership and service catalogue management;
improving end-to-end service design and transition controls;
integrating Agile, DevOps, product, and operations ways of working;
improving customer experience and service-level performance; and
strengthening governance over outsourced or multi-supplier environments.

Governance, Service Management, and Operations
ITIL 4 includes governance as a formal part of the Service Value System. This is important because service management is not only an operational discipline; it also requires policy direction, prioritisation, risk oversight, and performance review.
A practical governance structure for ITIL 4 often includes:

a board or executive forum providing strategic direction;
a CIO or service executive accountable for service performance and value;
service owners accountable for individual end-to-end services;
practice owners accountable for the design and performance of practices such as incident, change enablement, or service level management;
operational teams responsible for execution; and
assurance, security, risk, and audit teams providing oversight and challenge.

Example: Stabilising Service Operations After Cloud Transformation
An organisation has completed a rapid cloud migration but now faces frequent incidents, unclear ownership, inconsistent changes, weak supplier coordination, and poor visibility of service performance.
A practical ITIL 4 response would include:

defining the critical business services and assigning service owners;
establishing service catalogue entries and service level targets;
strengthening incident, major incident, request, problem, and change enablement practices;
creating runbooks, knowledge articles, and event monitoring models;
clarifying relationships between internal platform teams and cloud suppliers;
setting up service review forums and improvement backlogs; and
measuring restoration time, change success, major incident frequency, and customer satisfaction.

The result is not simply better support. It is a more controlled service operating model in which cloud platforms, suppliers, operations teams, and business stakeholders work through shared governance, shared service definitions, and measurable value outcomes.

The purpose of COBIT is to help enterprises:

create value from information and technology;
balance benefits realisation, risk optimisation, and resource optimisation;
align technology decisions with stakeholder needs and enterprise strategy;
establish clear governance accountabilities;
define management objectives, controls, and performance measures; and
provide a common language between the board, executives, auditors, risk, architecture, security, and operational teams.

COBIT is especially useful when an organisation needs a structured governance model across multiple technology and business functions. Typical situations include:

enterprise-wide IT governance transformation;
strengthening board oversight of digital and technology investment;
integrating risk, compliance, security, architecture, and service management;
improving auditability and control maturity;
rationalising overlapping frameworks and standards;
creating a governance layer above delivery frameworks such as PRINCE2, MSP, Agile, DevOps, or ITIL; and
supporting regulated or high-dependability environments.

Governance Components
A COBIT governance system is not made only of processes. It includes several interdependent components:

processes;
organisational structures;
principles, policies, and procedures;
information flows and items;
culture, ethics, and behaviour;
people, skills, and competencies; and
services, infrastructure, and applications.

This is one of COBIT’s strengths: it connects formal process design with the human, structural, and informational conditions needed for governance to work.

Design Factors
COBIT 2019 introduces design factors to tailor the governance system. Typical examples include:

enterprise strategy;
enterprise goals;
risk profile;
I&T-related issues;
threat landscape;
compliance requirements;
role of IT within the enterprise;
sourcing model;
implementation methods;
technology adoption strategy; and
enterprise size.

These design factors help avoid implementing every objective with equal depth. Instead, the organisation can decide where to focus more governance effort.

How to Read the Process Model
The COBIT process model should be used as a governance map rather than a rigid sequence. The objectives describe what must be governed or managed; they do not force a single operating design. For example:

APO02 and APO05 connect strategy with portfolio choices;
APO03 connects architecture to standards and future-state design;
BAI01 and BAI11 help structure delivery change through programmes and projects;
DSS02, DSS03, and DSS04 support reliable service operations and resilience;
MEA02, MEA03, and MEA04 provide the assurance layer needed for control, compliance, and audit.

Typical COBIT Artefact Families
COBIT does not prescribe one mandatory document pack for every organisation. Instead, artefacts are produced as evidence that governance decisions, management practices, controls, and oversight activities are functioning. A practical COBIT implementation usually generates artefacts across the following families:

governance and decision-rights documents;
strategy, architecture, and operating model artefacts;
portfolio, programme, project, and change artefacts;
risk, control, security, and compliance artefacts;
service management and operational artefacts; and
performance, assurance, and audit artefacts.

Management of Portfolios (MoP) is a portfolio management framework originally developed by the UK Cabinet Office and now positioned within the wider PRINCE2 / PeopleCert portfolio. It provides a structured approach to selecting, prioritising, governing, balancing, and controlling an organisation’s portfolio of change initiatives so that investment decisions remain aligned with strategy, capacity, risk appetite, and expected benefits.
MoP is concerned with doing the right change, not merely delivering change correctly. Where project and programme methods focus on execution, MoP focuses on whether the organisation is investing in the right mix of initiatives and whether those initiatives continue to deserve funding and executive attention.



The purpose of MoP is to help organisations:

translate strategy into an actionable change portfolio;
prioritise initiatives based on value, risk, urgency, and strategic contribution;
balance the portfolio across short-term and long-term needs;
control investment decisions using consistent governance and evidence;
monitor portfolio performance, benefits, dependencies, and overall affordability; and
stop, defer, reshape, or accelerate initiatives when business conditions change.




MoP is most useful when an organisation:

runs many projects and programmes at the same time;
needs clearer investment governance and prioritisation;
struggles with overcommitment, duplication, or fragmented decision-making;
wants to link strategy, funding, benefits, and delivery capacity;
needs an enterprise view of risk, interdependency, and change saturation; or
wants to improve board-level transparency over change investment.




MoP is particularly relevant for:

board members and executive committees;
CIOs, CFOs, COOs, and transformation leaders;
enterprise PMOs and portfolio offices;
portfolio managers and investment governance teams;
programme sponsors and business change leaders;
strategy, finance, risk, and architecture functions; and
senior managers involved in funding, assurance, and prioritisation decisions.




MoP provides a governance model for turning strategic intent into a managed portfolio of investments. It usually operates through two interacting management cycles:

a portfolio definition cycle, which decides what should enter, remain in, or leave the portfolio; and
a portfolio delivery cycle, which monitors progress, performance, risk, dependency, and benefits across the approved portfolio.

Together, these cycles help the organisation continuously answer five core questions:

Are we investing in the right things?
Are we investing at the right level?
Do we have the capacity and capability to deliver?
Are we obtaining the expected benefits and value?
Should we continue, stop, defer, or rebalance current investments?

